r="2.1"/><circle cx="242.0" cy="144.5" r="2.1"/><circle cx="254.0" cy="127.3" r="2.1"/><circle cx="212.5" cy="121.2" r="2.1"/><circle cx="255.7" cy="135.7" r="2.1"/><circle cx="229.6" cy="135.1" r="2.1"/><circle cx="277.9" cy="121.5" r="2.1"/></g><text class="lab" x="64" y="12" text-anchor="middle">DV</text><text class="lab" x="159" y="12" text-anchor="middle">IV&#8321;</text><text class="lab" x="254" y="12" text-anchor="middle">IV&#8322;</text><text class="lab ok" x="64" y="248">DV vs IV: want linear</text><text class="lab bad" x="300" y="248" text-anchor="end">IV vs IV: do not want linear</text>','Diagonal panels are distributions; off-diagonal are pairwise scatters. The asymmetry is the point &mdash; a linear pattern in a DV-versus-IV panel is what you want, the same pattern in an IV-versus-IV panel is multicollinearity. In practice violations get caught statistically, not by eye.',ARRAY['QM.LM1.c']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM2.01','quant','Variation decomposition','A full-width bar labelled SST splitting into a wider explained portion SSR and a narrower residual portion SSE.','0 0 420 160','<rect class="bar-t" x="24" y="24" width="372" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="210" y="41" text-anchor="middle">SST &#8212; total variation in Y</text><path class="edge" d="M120 50 V70" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M300 50 V70" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect class="bar-r" x="24" y="70" width="230" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="139" y="87" text-anchor="middle">SSR &#8212; explained</text><rect class="bar-e" x="258" y="70" width="138" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="327" y="87" text-anchor="middle">SSE &#8212; residual</text><text class="lab" x="24" y="118">R&#178; = SSR / SST</text><text class="lab" x="24" y="134" fill="var(--cyan)">here R&#178; = 230/372 = 0.62</text><text class="lab wn" x="396" y="118" text-anchor="end">adding any IV shrinks SSE</text><text class="lab wn" x="396" y="134" text-anchor="end">so R&#178; can never fall</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','R&#178; is just the left share of this bar. Because any extra regressor can only move variation from SSE to SSR, R&#178; is monotonic in the number of IVs and therefore useless for choosing between models. Adjusted R&#178;, AIC and BIC exist to penalise that.',ARRAY['QM.LM2.a']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM2.02','quant','Model selection criteria','Two curves against number of independent variables: R squared rising and flattening, adjusted R squared rising then turning down.','0 0 470 175','<line class="grid" x1="40" y1="28" x2="40" y2="128"/><line class="grid" x1="40" y1="128" x2="230" y2="128"/><text class="lab" x="135" y="143" text-anchor="middle">number of IVs</text><text class="lab" x="24" y="78" transform="rotate(-90 24 78)" text-anchor="middle">criterion</text><path d="M40.0 97.3 L42.7 94.7 L45.4 92.2 L48.1 89.9 L50.9 87.7 L53.6 85.5 L56.3 83.6 L59.0 81.7 L61.7 79.9 L64.4 78.2 L67.1 76.6 L69.9 75.1 L72.6 73.6 L75.3 72.2 L78.0 70.9 L80.7 69.7 L83.4 68.5 L86.1 67.4 L88.9 66.4 L91.6 65.4 L94.3 64.5 L97.0 63.6 L99.7 62.7 L102.4 61.9 L105.1 61.2 L107.9 60.4 L110.6 59.8 L113.3 59.1 L116.0 58.5 L118.7 57.9 L121.4 57.4 L124.1 56.8 L126.9 56.4 L129.6 55.9 L132.3 55.4 L135.0 55.0 L137.7 54.6 L140.4 54.2 L143.1 53.9 L145.9 53.5 L148.6 53.2 L151.3 52.9 L154.0 52.6 L156.7 52.3 L159.4 52.1 L162.1 51.8 L164.9 51.6 L167.6 51.4 L170.3 51.2 L173.0 51.0 L175.7 50.8 L178.4 50.6 L181.1 50.4 L183.9 50.3 L186.6 50.1 L189.3 50.0 L192.0 49.8 L194.7 49.7 L197.4 49.6 L200.1 49.5 L202.9 49.4 L205.6 49.3 L208.3 49.2 L211.0 49.1 L213.7 49.0 L216.4 48.9 L219.1 48.8 L221.9 48.7 L224.6 48.7 L227.3 48.6 L230.0 48.5" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.8" fill="none"/><path d="M40.0 99.2 L42.7 96.5 L45.4 93.9 L48.1 91.5 L50.9 89.3 L53.6 87.3 L56.3 85.3 L59.0 83.6 L61.7 81.9 L64.4 80.4 L67.1 78.9 L69.9 77.6 L72.6 76.4 L75.3 75.3 L78.0 74.2 L80.7 73.3 L83.4 72.4 L86.1 71.6 L88.9 70.9 L91.6 70.2 L94.3 69.6 L97.0 69.1 L99.7 68.6 L102.4 68.1 L105.1 67.7 L107.9 67.4 L110.6 67.1 L113.3 66.8 L116.0 66.6 L118.7 66.4 L121.4 66.3 L124.1 66.1 L126.9 66.0 L129.6 66.0 L132.3 65.9 L135.0 65.9 L137.7 65.9 L140.4 65.9 L143.1 66.0 L145.9 66.1 L148.6 66.1 L151.3 66.2 L154.0 66.4 L156.7 66.5 L159.4 66.6 L162.1 66.8 L164.9 67.0 L167.6 67.2 L170.3 67.4 L173.0 67.6 L175.7 67.8 L178.4 68.0 L181.1 68.3 L183.9 68.5 L186.6 68.8 L189.3 69.0 L192.0 69.3 L194.7 69.6 L197.4 69.8 L200.1 70.1 L202.9 70.4 L205.6 70.7 L208.3 71.0 L211.0 71.3 L213.7 71.6 L216.4 71.9 L219.1 72.3 L221.9 72.6 L224.6 72.9 L227.3 73.2 L230.0 73.6" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text class="lab" x="236" y="52" fill="var(--cyan)">R&#178; &#8212; never falls</text><text class="lab" x="236" y="70" fill="var(--amber)">adjusted R&#178; &#8212; can fall</text><line class="grid" x1="286" y1="90" x2="286" y2="150" stroke-dasharray="2 3"/><text class="lab" x="300" y="100">AIC &#8212; penalty 2(k+1)</text><text class="lab" x="300" y="116">BIC &#8212; penalty ln(n)(k+1)</text><text class="lab wn" x="300" y="134">ln(n) &#62; 2 for n &#62; 7,</text><text class="lab wn" x="300" y="147">so BIC punishes harder</text><text class="lab" x="40" y="160">AIC for prediction &#183; BIC for goodness of fit &#183; lower is better for both</text>','Adjusted R&#178; falls when an added variable has |t| &lt; 1 &mdash; that is the whole rule. AIC is the lighter penalty and suits prediction; BIC penalises more heavily because ln(n) exceeds 2 for any realistic sample, and suits picking the true model.',ARRAY['QM.LM2.a']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM2.03','quant','Joint hypothesis test','Unrestricted model above, restricted model below with two coefficients dropped, and the F statistic formed from the difference in residual sum of squares.','0 0 480 170','<rect class="node-c" x="20" y="24" width="190" height="42" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="115" y="43" text-anchor="middle">Unrestricted</text><text class="nm" x="115" y="55" text-anchor="middle">Y = b&#8320;+b&#8321;X&#8321;+b&#8322;X&#8322;+b&#8323;X&#8323;+b&#8324;X&#8324;</text><rect class="node" x="20" y="92" width="190" height="42" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="115" y="111" text-anchor="middle">Restricted (nested)</text><text class="nm" x="115" y="123" text-anchor="middle">Y = b&#8320;+b&#8321;X&#8321;+b&#8322;X&#8322;</text><path class="edge" d="M118 66 V92" marker-end="url(#a)"/><text class="el" x="126" y="82">drop X&#8323;, X&#8324;</text><path class="edge" d="M210 79 H250" marker-end="url(#c)"/><text class="nt" x="262" y="66">F = (SSE&#8341; &#8722; SSE&#8340;) / q</text><line class="grid" x1="262" y1="74" x2="424" y2="74"/><text class="nt" x="262" y="90">SSE&#8340; / (n &#8722; k &#8722; 1)</text><text class="lab" x="262" y="112">q = number of restrictions = 2</text><text class="lab bad" x="20" y="152">H&#8320;: b&#8323; = b&#8324; = 0 &#8212; reject means at least one belongs, not that both do</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','The restricted model must be nested inside the unrestricted one. Rejecting H&#8320; says at least one dropped coefficient is non-zero &mdash; it never tells you which, and a common error is reporting that both matter.',ARRAY['QM.LM2.b']::text[],'generated'),
