1.6 L151.7 110.3 L154.7 108.9 L157.6 107.5 L160.5 106.0 L163.5 104.5 L166.4 102.8 L169.4 101.1 L172.3 99.4 L175.3 97.5 L178.2 95.6 L181.1 93.6 L184.1 91.5 L187.0 89.4 L190.0 87.1 L192.9 84.7 L195.9 82.2 L198.8 79.6 L201.7 76.9 L204.7 74.1 L207.6 71.2 L210.6 68.1 L213.5 64.9 L216.5 61.5 L219.4 58.0 L222.3 54.4 L225.3 50.6 L228.2 46.6 L231.2 42.5 L234.1 38.1 L237.1 33.6 L240.0 28.9" stroke="var(--red)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/><path d="M34.0 28.2 L36.9 32.5 L39.9 36.5 L42.8 40.3 L45.8 43.9 L48.7 47.4 L51.7 50.6 L54.6 53.7 L57.5 56.7 L60.5 59.5 L63.4 62.1 L66.4 64.6 L69.3 67.0 L72.3 69.2 L75.2 71.2 L78.1 73.2 L81.1 75.0 L84.0 76.7 L87.0 78.3 L89.9 79.8 L92.9 81.2 L95.8 82.5 L98.7 83.6 L101.7 84.7 L104.6 85.6 L107.6 86.5 L110.5 87.2 L113.5 87.9 L116.4 88.5 L119.3 88.9 L122.3 89.3 L125.2 89.6 L128.2 89.8 L131.1 89.9 L134.1 90.0 L137.0 89.9 L139.9 89.8 L142.9 89.5 L145.8 89.2 L148.8 88.8 L151.7 88.3 L154.7 87.7 L157.6 87.0 L160.5 86.2 L163.5 85.3 L166.4 84.3 L169.4 83.2 L172.3 82.1 L175.3 80.8 L178.2 79.4 L181.1 77.9 L184.1 76.3 L187.0 74.6 L190.0 72.7 L192.9 70.8 L195.9 68.7 L198.8 66.5 L201.7 64.2 L204.7 61.7 L207.6 59.1 L210.6 56.4 L213.5 53.5 L216.5 50.4 L219.4 47.2 L222.3 43.9 L225.3 40.3 L228.2 36.6 L231.2 32.7 L234.1 28.6 L237.1 24.3 L240.0 19.8" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/><text class="lab wn" x="46" y="42">bias (underfit)</text><text class="lab bad" x="200" y="52" text-anchor="end">variance (overfit)</text><text class="lab" x="133.3" y="22" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--cyan)">optimal</text>','In-sample error keeps falling with complexity; out-of-sample error does not. Linear models sit left and carry bias error, non-linear models sit right and carry variance error. Only the cyan curve is observable on held-out data.',ARRAY['QM.LM6.b']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM6.03','quant','Data partitioning and k-fold cross validation','Top: a data set split into training, validation and test portions. Below: four rounds of k-fold cross validation with a different fold used for validation each round.','0 0 620 200','<rect class="bar-r" x="20" y="24" width="300" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="170" y="41" text-anchor="middle">Training</text><rect class="bar-t" x="324" y="24" width="130" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="389" y="41" text-anchor="middle">Validation</text><rect class="bar-e" x="458" y="24" width="130" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="523" y="41" text-anchor="middle">Test</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="66">in-sample</text><text class="lab" x="324" y="66">out-of-sample &#8212; both must come from the same domain as training</text><text class="ttl" x="20" y="98">k-fold: shuffle training data into k parts, rotate which one validates</text><rect class="fold-v" x="20" y="110" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="78" y="110" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="136" y="110" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="194" y="110" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="252" y="110" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><text class="lab" x="322" y="121">x</text><rect class="fold-t" x="20" y="130" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-v" x="78" y="130" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="136" y="130" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="194" y="130" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="252" y="130" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><text class="lab" x="322" y="141">x</text><rect class="fold-t" x="20" y="150" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="78" y="150" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-v" x="136" y="150" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="194" y="150" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="252" y="150" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><text class="lab" x="322" y="161">x</text><rect class="fold-t" x="20" y="170" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="78" y="170" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="136" y="170" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-v" x="194" y="170" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><rect class="fold-t" x="252" y="170" width="52" height="14" rx="1"/><text class="lab" x="322" y="181">x</text><text class="lab" x="322" y="121">round 1</text><text class="lab" x="322" y="141">round 2</text><text class="lab" x="322" y="161">round 3</text><text class="lab" x="322" y="181">round 4</text><rect class="fold-v" x="404" y="110" width="14" height="12" rx="1"/><text class="lab" x="424" y="120">validation fold</text><rect class="fold-t" x="404" y="130" width="14" height="12" rx="1"/><text class="lab" x="424" y="140">training folds</text><text class="lab" x="404" y="164">k typically 5 to 10</text><text class="lab wn" x="404" y="180">every observation validates exactly once</text>','Validation tunes the model, test is touched once at the end. K-fold matters when data is scarce: every observation serves as validation exactly once, so you get a stability estimate without permanently surrendering rows to a holdout.',ARRAY['QM.LM6.b']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM6.04','quant','Penalised regression (LASSO)','The LASSO objective split into fit and penalty terms, alongside a bar chart of coefficients where several have been flattened to zero.','0 0 540 180','<text class="nt" x="30" y="40">min &#160; &#931;&#949;&#178;&#7522; &#160; + &#160; &#955; &#931;|b&#7523;|</text><path class="edge" d="M92 50 V72" marker-end="url(#a)"/><text class="lab" x="66" y="86">fit term</text><path class="edge" d="M176 50 V72" marker-end="url(#a)"/><text class="lab wn" x="150" y="86">penalty term</text><text class="lab" x="30" y="112">a coefficient survives only if it cuts &#931;&#949;&#178; by more than it adds in penalty</text><text class="lab" x="30" y="128">&#955; is a hyperparameter &#8212; set by you, not estimated</text><rect class="coef-on" x="312" y="63" width="14" height="41" rx="1"/><rect class="coef-on" x="334" y="79" width="14" height="25" rx="1"/><rect class="coef-off" x="356" y="102" width="14" height="2" rx="1"/><rect class="coef-on" x="378" y="71" width="14" height="33" rx="1"/><rect class="coef-off" x="400" y="103" width="14" height="2" rx="1"/><rect class="coef-on" x="422" y="87" width="14" height="17" rx="1"/><rect class="coef-off" x="444" y="104" width="14" height="2" rx="1"/><rect class="coef-on" x="466" y="76" width="14" height="28" rx="1"/><line class="grid" x1="306" y1="106" x2="500" y2="106"/><text class="lab" x="306" y="124">coefficients after LASSO</text><text class="lab bad" x="306" y="138">flattened to zero = dropped</text><text class="ttl" x="306" y="34">dimension reduction by elimination</text><text class="lab" x="30" y="162">shrinking weak coefficients to exactly zero is what makes LASSO a feature selector, not just a shrinker</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','The penalty grows with the number of included features, so a coefficient must earn its place. Unlike ridge-style shrinkage, LASSO drives weak coefficients to exactly zero, which is why it performs feature selection rather than merely damping.',ARRAY['QM.LM6.c']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM6.05','quant','Support vector machine','Two classes of points separated by a boundary line with a shaded margin, and two boundary-adjacent points circled as support vectors.','0 0 300 190','<line class="grid" x1="24" y1="18" x2="24" y2="142"/><line class="grid" x1="24" y1="142" x2="264" y2="142"/><path d="M40 138 L246 34 L246 62 L40 166 Z" fill="var(--cyan)" opacity=".10"/><line x1="40" y1="152" x2="246" y2="48" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="40" y1="138" x2="246" y2="34" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/><line x1="40" y1="166" x2="246" y2="62" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/><circle cx="64" cy="42" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="92" cy="30" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="120" cy="48" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="150" cy="36" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="176" cy="26" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="206" cy="40" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="232" cy="28" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="88" cy="58" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="140" cy="62" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="196" cy="60" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="120" cy="26" r="2.6" class="pt"/><circle cx="168" cy="50" r="2.6" class="pt"/><path d="M45 123 L51 123 L48 117 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M71 135 L77 135 L74 129 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M101 127 L107 127 L104 121 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M127 139 L133 139 L130 133 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M155 131 L161 131 L158 125 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M181 141 L187 141 L184 135 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M209 133 L215 133 L212 127 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M57 107 L63 107 L60 101 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M109 109 L115 109 L112 103 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M163 115 L169 115 L166 109 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M87 119 L93 119 L90 113 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M137 121 L143 121 L140 115 Z" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="96" cy="112" r="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width="1.2"/><circle cx="164" cy="70" r="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text class="lab wn" x="24" y="164">circled = support vectors, the only points that define the boundary</text><text class="lab" x="24" y="178">margin is maximised, so the boundary sits furthest from both classes</text>','SVM picks the separating line furthest from every observation, which maximises the chance of classifying new data correctly. Only the points on the margin edge matter &mdash; move a far-away point and nothing changes, which is why SVM is resilient to outliers and correlated features.',ARRAY['QM.LM6.c']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM6.06','quant','k-nearest neighbour','The same new observation classified two ways: with k equals one it takes the nearest class, with k equals five the majority within a wider radius flips the answer.','0 0 460 175','<g><text class="ttl" x="24" y="16">k = 1</text><line class="grid" x1="24" y1="22" x2="24" y2="134"/><line class="grid" x1="24" y1="134" x2="204" y2="134"/><circle cx="60" cy="48" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="88" cy="36" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="120" cy="54" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="150" cy="40" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="176" cy="60" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="96" cy="70" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="140" cy="74" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="70" cy="90" r="2.5" class="pt"/><path d="M53 115 L59 115 L56 109 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M81 123 L87 123 L84 117 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M109 111 L115 111 L112 105 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M143 121 L149 121 L146 115 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M169 107 L175 107 L172 101 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M97 101 L103 101 L100 95 Z" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="106" cy="86" r="4" fill="var(--amber)"/><circle cx="106" cy="86" r="17" fill="none" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/><text class="lab ok" x="24" y="152">nearest is a circle &#8594; class A</text></g><g transform="translate(240,0)"><text class="ttl" x="24" y="16">k = 5</text><line class="grid" x1="24" y1="22" x2="24" y2="134"/><line class="grid" x1="24" y1="134" x2="204" y2="134"/><circle cx="60" cy="48" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="88" cy="36" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="120" cy="54" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="150" cy="40" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="176" cy="60" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="96" cy="70" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="140" cy="74" r="2.5" class="pt"/><circle cx="70" cy="90" r="2.5" class="pt"/><path d="M53 115 L59 115 L56 109 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M81 123 L87 123 L84 117 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M109 111 L115 111 L112 105 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M143 121 L149 121 L146 115 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M169 107 L175 107 L172 101 Z" class="pt-r"/><path d="M97 101 L103 101 L100 95 Z" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="106" cy="86" r="4" fill="var(--amber)"/><circle cx="106" cy="86" r="34" fill="none" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/><text class="lab bad" x="24" y="152">majority now triangles &#8594; class B</text></g><text class="lab wn" x="24" y="170">k too small: sensitive to local outliers &#183; k too large: averages away the idea of nearest</text>','Same data, same point, opposite classification. That is the whole difficulty with KNN &mdash; k is chosen by you and it changes the answer. It also needs a defensible distance metric, and it degrades badly with irrelevant or correlated features.',ARRAY['QM.LM6.c']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM6.07','quant','CART and random forest','Left: a decision tree splitting on feature thresholds down to terminal class labels. Right: three trees built on different random feature subsets feeding a majority vote.','0 0 640 230','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="16">Classification and regression tree</text><rect class="node-c" x="112" y="26" width="86" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="155" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Root</text><text class="nm" x="155" y="49" text-anchor="middle">X&#8322; &#60; 0.4?</text><path class="edge edge-y" d="M140 52 L96 76" marker-end="url(#g)"/><text class="el" x="106" y="70">yes</text><path class="edge edge-n" d="M170 52 L214 76" marker-end="url(#m)"/><text class="el" x="200" y="70">no</text><rect class="node" x="54" y="76" width="84" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="96" y="92" text-anchor="middle">X&#8325; &#60; 12?</text><rect class="node" x="176" y="76" width="84" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="218" y="92" text-anchor="middle">X&#8321; &#60; 7?</text><path class="edge" d="M82 102 L58 126" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M112 102 L136 126" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M204 102 L180 126" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M234 102 L258 126" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect class="node-ok" x="30" y="126" width="56" height="24" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="58" y="142" text-anchor="middle">A</text><rect class="node-ok" x="108" y="126" width="56" height="24" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="136" y="142" text-anchor="middle">B</text><rect class="node-ok" x="152" y="126" width="56" height="24" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="180" y="142" text-anchor="middle">B</text><rect class="node-ok" x="230" y="126" width="56" height="24" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="258" y="142" text-anchor="middle">A</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="176">each node picks one feature and a cutoff by minimising MSE or Gini</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="192">terminal node predicts the majority class in that partition</text><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="208">overfits easily &#8212; cap depth, set minimum node size, or prune</text><line class="grid" x1="330" y1="12" x2="330" y2="216"/><text class="ttl" x="352" y="16">Random forest</text><g transform="translate(352,30)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="72" height="52" rx="2"/><circle cx="36" cy="12" r="3" fill="var(--faint)"/><line x1="36" y1="15" x2="20" y2="28" class="edge"/><line x1="36" y1="15" x2="52" y2="28" class="edge"/><circle cx="20" cy="31" r="3" fill="var(--faint)"/><circle cx="52" cy="31" r="3" fill="var(--faint)"/><text class="nm" x="36" y="47" text-anchor="middle">features 1,2</text></g><g transform="translate(444,30)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="72" height="52" rx="2"/><circle cx="36" cy="12" r="3" fill="var(--faint)"/><line x1="36" y1="15" x2="20" y2="28" class="edge"/><line x1="36" y1="15" x2="52" y2="28" class="edge"/><circle cx="20" cy="31" r="3" fill="var(--faint)"/><circle cx="52" cy="31" r="3" fill="var(--faint)"/><text class="nm" x="36" y="47" text-anchor="middle">features 3,4</text></g><g transform="translate(536,30)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="72" height="52" rx="2"/><circle cx="36" cy="12" r="3" fill="var(--faint)"/><line x1="36" y1="15" x2="20" y2="28" class="edge"/><line x1="36" y1="15" x2="52" y2="28" class="edge"/><circle cx="20" cy="31" r="3" fill="var(--faint)"/><circle cx="52" cy="31" r="3" fill="var(--faint)"/><text class="nm" x="36" y="47" text-anchor="middle">features 5,6</text></g><text class="lab" x="352" y="100">bootstrap the data, take a random subset of features per tree</text><text class="lab" x="352" y="116">so every tree is deliberately different</text><path class="edge" d="M388 82 V132 H500" marker-end="url(#c)"/><path class="edge" d="M572 82 V132 H520" marker-end="url(#c)"/><rect class="node-c" x="450" y="132" width="120" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="510" y="151" text-anchor="middle">Majority vote</text><text class="lab ok" x="352" y="184">errors cancel across trees, so noise falls relative to signal</text><text class="lab ok" x="352" y="200">this is what protects against the overfitting a single tree suffers</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','A single tree overfits because it keeps splitting until the partitions are pure. A random forest builds many deliberately different trees and votes; individual errors are uncorrelated so they cancel, which is why the ensemble generalises where one tree does not.',ARRAY['QM.LM6.c']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM6.08','quant','PCA and the scree plot','Left: correlated data with the first principal component along the direction of greatest spread and the second orthogonal to it. Right: a scree plot of declining variance shares with a cumulative line crossing the eighty five percent threshold.','0 0 560 190','<g><text class="ttl" x="26" y="16">Eigenvectors are new axes</text><line class="grid" x1="26" y1="22" x2="26" y2="132"/><line class="grid" x1="26" y1="132" x2="206" y2="132"/><g class="pt"><circle cx="115.0" cy="90.1" r="2.1"/><circle cx="83.7" cy="89.5" r="2.1"/><circle cx="83.8" cy="87.7" r="2.1"/><circle cx="90.7" cy="91.2" r="2.1"/><circle cx="99.0" cy="83.2" r="2.1"/><circle cx="139.7" cy="67.4" r="2.1"/><circle cx="186.9" cy="64.2" r="2.1"/><circle cx="86.8" cy="93.9" r="2.1"/><circle cx="104.0" cy="72.3" r="2.1"/><circle cx="79.4" cy="76.1" r="2.1"/><circle cx="78.5" cy="79.0" r="2.1"/><circle cx="202.2" cy="57.4" r="2.1"/><circle cx="137.2" cy="71.2" r="2.1"/><circle cx="58.2" cy="101.3" r="2.1"/><circle cx="154.3" cy="68.4" r="2.1"/><circle cx="49.5" cy="85.0" r="2.1"/><circle cx="36.5" cy="96.7" r="2.1"/><circle cx="95.3" cy="81.0" r="2.1"/><circle cx="179.0" cy="54.1" r="2.1"/><circle cx="45.4" cy="93.8" r="2.1"/><circle cx="88.3" cy="78.7" r="2.1"/><circle cx="137.8" cy="69.8" r="2.1"/><circle cx="137.0" cy="63.4" r="2.1"/><circle cx="102.1" cy="78.6" r="2.1"/><circle cx="97.6" cy="76.4" r="2.1"/><circle cx="44.4" cy="88.3" r="2.1"/><circle cx="149.5" cy="75.4" r="2.1"/><circle cx="99.3" cy="89.5" r="2.1"/><circle cx="198.2" cy="61.0" r="2.1"/><circle cx="93.2" cy="78.6" r="2.1"/><circle cx="190.3" cy="52.4" r="2.1"/><circle cx="194.9" cy="47.6" r="2.1"/><circle cx="179.3" cy="70.1" r="2.1"/><circle cx="182.9" cy="59.4" r="2.1"/><circle cx="153.9" cy="66.4" r="2.1"/><circle cx="135.5" cy="76.8" r="2.1"/><circle cx="158.9" cy="66.5" r="2.1"/><circle cx="177.4" cy="57.3" r="2.1"/><circle cx="128.3" cy="81.5" r="2.1"/><circle cx="72.3" cy="82.7" r="2.1"/><circle cx="70.6" cy="81.7" r="2.1"/><circle cx="52.4" cy="87.3" r="2.1"/><circle cx="187.4" cy="50.4" r="2.1"/><circle cx="42.2" cy="97.2" r="2.1"/><circle cx="133.7" cy="68.7" r="2.1"/><circle cx="49.1" cy="86.8" r="2.1"/></g><line x1="38" y1="118" x2="196" y2="40" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text class="lab" x="150" y="38" fill="var(--cyan)">PC1</text><line x1="104" y1="60" x2="132" y2="112" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width="1.3"/><text class="lab wn" x="136" y="108">PC2</text><text class="lab" x="26" y="152">PC1 captures the most spread; PC2 is orthogonal to it</text><text class="lab" x="26" y="166">composite variables, uncorrelated by construction</text></g><g transform="translate(300,0)"><text class="ttl" x="26" y="16">Scree plot</text><line class="grid" x1="26" y1="22" x2="26" y2="132"/><line class="grid" x1="26" y1="132" x2="226" y2="132"/><text class="lab" x="126" y="147" text-anchor="middle">principal component</text><text class="lab" x="10" y="77" transform="rotate(-90 10 77)" text-anchor="middle">% variance</text><rect class="barc" x="34" y="67" width="16" height="65"/><rect class="barc" x="58" y="110" width="16" height="22"/><rect class="barc" x="82" y="123" width="16" height="9"/><rect class="barc" x="106" y="128" width="16" height="4"/><rect class="barc" x="130" y="130" width="16" height="2"/><rect class="barc" x="154" y="131" width="16" height="1"/><rect class="barc" x="178" y="131" width="16" height="1"/><rect class="barc" x="202" y="132" width="16" height="0"/><path d="M42 66.89999999999999 L66 44.849999999999994 L90 35.39999999999999 L114 31.19999999999999 L138 29.099999999999994" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.4" fill="none"/><line x1="26" y1="37.5" x2="226" y2="37.5" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/><text class="lab" x="228" y="40.5" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--faint)">85&#8211;95%</text><text class="lab ok" x="26" y="166">keep 3 components, discard 5 features</text></g>','PCA replaces correlated features with uncorrelated composites. You keep however many eigenvectors reach 85&ndash;95% cumulative variance. The cost is interpretability &mdash; the components are combinations, so they no longer correspond to anything you can name.',ARRAY['QM.LM6.d']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM6.09','quant','k-means clustering','Three panels: randomly placed centroids, observations assigned to the nearest centroid, then centroids recomputed at cluster means.','0 0 640 175','<g transform="translate(16,18)"><text class="ttl" x="0" y="-4">1. Random centroids</text><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="104"/><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="104" x2="132" y2="104"/><circle cx="28" cy="30" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="46" cy="22" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="38" cy="46" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="60" cy="36" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="92" cy="26" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="104" cy="42" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="88" cy="50" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="112" cy="30" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="40" cy="80" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="58" cy="88" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="34" cy="94" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="66" cy="74" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="96" cy="84" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><circle cx="110" cy="92" r="2.3" class="ptc"/><path d="M46 54 L50 50 L54 54 L50 58 Z" fill="var(--cyan)"/><path d="M80 44 L84 40 L88 44 L84 48 Z" fill="var(--amber)"/><path d="M66 80 L70 76 L74 80 L70 84 Z" fill="var(--red)"/></g><g transform="translate(172,18)"><text class="ttl" x="0" y="-4">2. Assign to nearest</text><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="104"/><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="104" x2="132" y2="104"/><circle cx="28" cy="30" r="2.3" class="pt"/><circle cx="46" cy="22" r="2.3" class="pt"/><circle cx="38" cy="46" r="2.3" class="pt"/><circle cx="60" cy="36" r="2.3" class="pt"/><circle cx="92" cy="26" r="2.3" class="pt-w"/><circle cx="104" cy="42" r="2.3" class="pt-w"/><circle cx="88" cy="50" r="2.3" class="pt-w"/><circle cx="112" cy="30" r="2.3" class="pt-w"/><circle cx="40" cy="80" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="58" cy="88" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="34" cy="94" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="66" cy="74" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="96" cy="84" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="110" cy="92" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><path d="M46 54 L50 50 L54 54 L50 58 Z" fill="var(--cyan)"/><path d="M80 44 L84 40 L88 44 L84 48 Z" fill="var(--amber)"/><path d="M66 80 L70 76 L74 80 L70 84 Z" fill="var(--red)"/></g><g transform="translate(328,18)"><text class="ttl" x="0" y="-4">3. Recompute, repeat</text><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="104"/><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="104" x2="132" y2="104"/><circle cx="28" cy="30" r="2.3" class="pt"/><circle cx="46" cy="22" r="2.3" class="pt"/><circle cx="38" cy="46" r="2.3" class="pt"/><circle cx="60" cy="36" r="2.3" class="pt"/><circle cx="92" cy="26" r="2.3" class="pt-w"/><circle cx="104" cy="42" r="2.3" class="pt-w"/><circle cx="88" cy="50" r="2.3" class="pt-w"/><circle cx="112" cy="30" r="2.3" class="pt-w"/><circle cx="40" cy="80" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="58" cy="88" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="34" cy="94" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="66" cy="74" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="96" cy="84" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><circle cx="110" cy="92" r="2.3" class="pt-r"/><path d="M39 34 L43 30 L47 34 L43 38 Z" fill="var(--cyan)"/><path d="M95 37 L99 33 L103 37 L99 41 Z" fill="var(--amber)"/><path d="M63 85 L67 81 L71 85 L67 89 Z" fill="var(--red)"/></g><text class="lab" x="16" y="152">k is chosen by the researcher &#8212; the algorithm cannot tell you how many clusters exist</text><text class="lab ok" x="16" y="167">iteration stops when no observation changes cluster &#183; scales to hundreds of millions of points</text>','Assign, recompute, repeat until nothing moves. Two things are on you rather than the algorithm: k itself, and the distance metric that defines similar. Both change the answer, and neither is validated by the procedure.',ARRAY['QM.LM6.d']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM6.10','quant','Hierarchical clustering and dendrograms','A dendrogram with merge heights and a horizontal cut line producing two clusters, alongside a comparison of agglomerative and divisive directions.','0 0 560 195','<text class="ttl" x="24" y="16">Dendrogram &#8212; height is distance at merge</text><line class="grid" x1="40" y1="24" x2="40" y2="144"/><line class="grid" x1="40" y1="144" x2="290" y2="144"/><text class="lab" x="24" y="84" transform="rotate(-90 24 84)" text-anchor="middle">distance</text><text class="lab" x="56" y="158" text-anchor="middle">A</text><text class="lab" x="88" y="158" text-anchor="middle">B</text><text class="lab" x="120" y="158" text-anchor="middle">C</text><text class="lab" x="152" y="158" text-anchor="middle">D</text><text class="lab" x="184" y="158" text-anchor="middle">E</text><text class="lab" x="216" y="158" text-anchor="middle">F</text><text class="lab" x="248" y="158" text-anchor="middle">G</text><path d="M56 144 V120 H88 V144" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none"/><path d="M120 144 V128 H152 V144" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none"/><path d="M184 144 V132 H216 V144" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none"/><path d="M72 120 V96 H136 V128" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none"/><path d="M200 132 V104 H248 V144" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none"/><path d="M104 96 V64 H224 V104" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/><line x1="40" y1="88" x2="290" y2="88" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text class="lab wn" x="294" y="92">cut here &#8594; 2 clusters</text><text class="lab" x="24" y="176">cutting at a different height gives a different number of clusters &#8212; no k required up front</text><line class="grid" x1="330" y1="12" x2="330" y2="180"/><text class="ttl" x="352" y="16">Two directions</text><path class="edge edge-y" d="M360 40 H520" marker-end="url(#g)"/><text class="nt" x="352" y="34">Agglomerative &#8212; bottom-up</text><text class="lab" x="352" y="56">each point starts alone, merge nearest pairs</text><text class="lab" x="352" y="70">builds from local patterns</text><path class="edge edge-n" d="M520 108 H360" marker-end="url(#m)"/><text class="nt" x="352" y="102">Divisive &#8212; top-down</text><text class="lab" x="352" y="124">one cluster splits repeatedly</text><text class="lab" x="352" y="138">begins from global structure</text><text class="lab wn" x="352" y="166">both end at the same extremes; the intermediate groupings differ</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','Height is the distance at which two groups merged, so a tall join means dissimilar groups. Unlike k-means you do not commit to a cluster count in advance &mdash; you cut the tree wherever the merge heights jump.',ARRAY['QM.LM6.d']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM6.11','quant','Neural network anatomy','A four node input layer fully connected to a five node hidden layer and a single output node, with a sigmoid activation curve alongside.','0 0 620 210','<circle cx="60" cy="40" r="9" class="nn-in"/><circle cx="60" cy="74" r="9" class="nn-in"/><circle cx="60" cy="108" r="9" class="nn-in"/><circle cx="60" cy="142" r="9" class="nn-in"/><circle cx="200" cy="28" r="9" class="nn-hid"/><circle cx="200" cy="60" r="9" class="nn-hid"/><circle cx="200" cy="92" r="9" class="nn-hid"/><circle cx="200" cy="124" r="9" class="nn-hid"/><circle cx="200" cy="156" r="9" class="nn-hid"/><circle cx="330" cy="90" r="9" class="nn-out"/><line x1="69" y1="40" x2="191" y2="28" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="40" x2="191" y2="60" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="40" x2="191" y2="92" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="40" x2="191" y2="124" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="40" x2="191" y2="156" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="74" x2="191" y2="28" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="74" x2="191" y2="60" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="74" x2="191" y2="92" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="74" x2="191" y2="124" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="74" x2="191" y2="156" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="108" x2="191" y2="28" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="108" x2="191" y2="60" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="108" x2="191" y2="92" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="108" x2="191" y2="124" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="108" x2="191" y2="156" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="142" x2="191" y2="28" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="142" x2="191" y2="60" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="142" x2="191" y2="92" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="142" x2="191" y2="124" class="nn-link"/><line x1="69" y1="142" x2="191" y2="156" class="nn-link"/><line x1="209" y1="28" x2="321" y2="90" class="nn-link"/><line x1="209" y1="60" x2="321" y2="90" class="nn-link"/><line x1="209" y1="92" x2="321" y2="90" class="nn-link"/><line x1="209" y1="124" x2="321" y2="90" class="nn-link"/><line x1="209" y1="156" x2="321" y2="90" class="nn-link"/><text class="lab" x="60" y="18" text-anchor="middle">input</text><text class="lab" x="200" y="18" text-anchor="middle">hidden</text><text class="lab" x="330" y="18" text-anchor="middle">output</text><text class="lab wn" x="60" y="190" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text class="lab wn" x="200" y="190" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text class="lab wn" x="330" y="190" text-anchor="middle">1</text><path class="edge" d="M60 176 V166 M200 176 V166 M330 176 V166" stroke="var(--faint)"/><text class="lab wn" x="60" y="204">these three counts are the hyperparameters</text><line class="grid" x1="392" y1="12" x2="392" y2="196"/><text class="ttl" x="414" y="24">Inside one hidden node</text><text class="lab" x="414" y="46">1. receives a value from every preceding node</text><text class="lab" x="414" y="62">2. weights them and adds &#8212; summation operator</text><text class="lab" x="414" y="78">3. passes the sum through an activation function</text><line class="grid" x1="430" y1="92" x2="430" y2="166"/><line class="grid" x1="430" y1="166" x2="570" y2="166"/><path d="M430.0 165.5 L432.0 165.4 L434.0 165.3 L436.0 165.2 L438.0 165.1 L440.0 165.0 L442.0 164.8 L444.0 164.7 L446.0 164.5 L448.0 164.2 L450.0 164.0 L452.0 163.7 L454.0 163.3 L456.0 162.9 L458.0 162.5 L460.0 162.0 L462.0 161.4 L464.0 160.7 L466.0 160.0 L468.0 159.2 L470.0 158.2 L472.0 157.2 L474.0 156.0 L476.0 154.7 L478.0 153.3 L480.0 151.7 L482.0 150.0 L484.0 148.1 L486.0 146.1 L488.0 144.0 L490.0 141.7 L492.0 139.3 L494.0 136.8 L496.0 134.3 L498.0 131.6 L500.0 129.0 L502.0 126.4 L504.0 123.7 L506.0 121.2 L508.0 118.7 L510.0 116.3 L512.0 114.0 L514.0 111.9 L516.0 109.9 L518.0 108.0 L520.0 106.3 L522.0 104.7 L524.0 103.3 L526.0 102.0 L528.0 100.8 L530.0 99.8 L532.0 98.8 L534.0 98.0 L536.0 97.3 L538.0 96.6 L540.0 96.0 L542.0 95.5 L544.0 95.1 L546.0 94.7 L548.0 94.3 L550.0 94.0 L552.0 93.8 L554.0 93.5 L556.0 93.3 L558.0 93.2 L560.0 93.0 L562.0 92.9 L564.0 92.8 L566.0 92.7 L568.0 92.6 L570.0 92.5" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/><text class="lab" x="430" y="182">summation &#8594; bounded output</text><text class="lab" x="570" y="182" text-anchor="end">non-linear by design</text>','Input data is scaled to 0&ndash;1. Each hidden node sums its weighted inputs then passes the total through a non-linear activation function &mdash; that non-linearity is the entire reason neural nets handle relationships regression cannot. The layer counts are hyperparameters you set.',ARRAY['QM.LM6.a']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM7.01','quant','Data analysis project steps','Two parallel five-stage pipelines, one for structured data and one for textual data, with matching stages.','0 0 660 200','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="18">Structured data</text><rect class="node" x="20" y="28" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="76" y="43" text-anchor="middle">Conceptualise</text><text class="nm" x="76" y="55" text-anchor="middle">what output?</text><rect class="node" x="146" y="28" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="202" y="43" text-anchor="middle">Collect</text><text class="nm" x="202" y="55" text-anchor="middle">from where?</text><rect class="node" x="272" y="28" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="328" y="43" text-anchor="middle">Prepare</text><text class="nm" x="328" y="55" text-anchor="middle">cleanse, wrangle</text><rect class="node" x="398" y="28" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="454" y="43" text-anchor="middle">Explore</text><text class="nm" x="454" y="55" text-anchor="middle">EDA, features</text><rect class="node" x="524" y="28" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="580" y="43" text-anchor="middle">Train</text><text class="nm" x="580" y="55" text-anchor="middle">fit and tune</text><path class="edge" d="M132 45 H146" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M258 45 H272" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M384 45 H398" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M510 45 H524" marker-end="url(#a)"/><text class="ttl" x="20" y="106">Textual data</text><rect class="node-c" x="20" y="116" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="76" y="131" text-anchor="middle">Formulate</text><text class="nm" x="76" y="143" text-anchor="middle">inputs/outputs?</text><rect class="node-c" x="146" y="116" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="202" y="131" text-anchor="middle">Curate</text><text class="nm" x="202" y="143" text-anchor="middle">source the text</text><rect class="node-c" x="272" y="116" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="328" y="131" text-anchor="middle">Prepare</text><text class="nm" x="328" y="143" text-anchor="middle">clean, tokenise</text><rect class="node-c" x="398" y="116" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="454" y="131" text-anchor="middle">Explore</text><text class="nm" x="454" y="143" text-anchor="middle">what to look for</text><rect class="node-c" x="524" y="116" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="580" y="131" text-anchor="middle">Classify</text><text class="nm" x="580" y="143" text-anchor="middle">model output</text><path class="edge" d="M132 133 H146" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M258 133 H272" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M384 133 H398" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M510 133 H524" marker-end="url(#a)"/><text class="lab" x="20" y="176">same five stages; only the middle three change method</text><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="192">4 Vs of big data: volume, variety, velocity, veracity</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','The pipelines are structurally identical. Only preparation, exploration and training differ in method, which is why questions about text projects are usually answerable from the structured-data process plus knowledge of tokenisation.',ARRAY['QM.LM7.a']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM7.02','quant','Text preprocessing pipeline','A six stage text preprocessing chain ending in a document term matrix, with notes on feature selection methods below.','0 0 660 190','<g transform="translate(20,22)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="94" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="47" y="14" text-anchor="middle">Cleanse</text><text class="nm" x="47" y="25" text-anchor="middle">html, punctuation</text></g><g transform="translate(126,22)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="94" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="47" y="14" text-anchor="middle">Tokenise</text><text class="nm" x="47" y="25" text-anchor="middle">split into words</text></g><g transform="translate(232,22)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="94" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="47" y="14" text-anchor="middle">Lowercase</text><text class="nm" x="47" y="25" text-anchor="middle">normalise case</text></g><g transform="translate(338,22)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="94" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="47" y="14" text-anchor="middle">Stop words</text><text class="nm" x="47" y="25" text-anchor="middle">remove the, is, a</text></g><g transform="translate(444,22)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="94" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="47" y="14" text-anchor="middle">Stem / lemma</text><text class="nm" x="47" y="25" text-anchor="middle">analyz / analyze</text></g><g transform="translate(550,22)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="94" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="47" y="14" text-anchor="middle">BOW &#8594; DTM</text><text class="nm" x="47" y="25" text-anchor="middle">structured at last</text></g><path class="edge" d="M114 37 H126" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M220 37 H232" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M326 37 H338" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M432 37 H444" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M538 37 H550" marker-end="url(#a)"/><text class="lab" x="20" y="76">stemming chops to a root that may not be a word; lemmatisation returns a real word and costs more</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="92">both shrink the token count and reduce sparseness, which lowers model complexity</text><text class="ttl" x="20" y="122">Feature selection &#8212; drop noisy tokens</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="142">document frequency &#8212; share of documents containing the token</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="158">chi-square &#8212; tokens associated with a specific class</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="174">mutual information &#8212; 0 if spread evenly across classes, 1 if unique to one</text><rect class="node-r" x="392" y="112" width="248" height="66" rx="2"/><text class="nt bad" x="406" y="132">most frequent tokens &#8594; underfitting</text><text class="nt bad" x="406" y="152">most sparse tokens &#8594; overfitting</text><text class="lab" x="406" y="170">both extremes get removed</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','The pipeline exists to turn text into a structured matrix. Feature selection then trims both ends of the frequency distribution: tokens appearing everywhere carry no signal and cause underfitting, tokens appearing once cause overfitting.',ARRAY['QM.LM7.e','QM.LM7.f']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM7.03','quant','Confusion matrix','Two by two confusion matrix with true and false positives and negatives, annotated with precision reading across the top row and recall reading down the first column.','0 0 620 215','<text class="lab" x="130" y="24" text-anchor="middle">actual 1</text><text class="lab" x="238" y="24" text-anchor="middle">actual 0</text><text class="lab" x="66" y="60" text-anchor="end">predicted 1</text><text class="lab" x="66" y="122" text-anchor="end">predicted 0</text><rect class="cell-tp" x="76" y="32" width="108" height="52" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="130" y="56" text-anchor="middle">TP</text><text class="nm" x="130" y="70" text-anchor="middle">correct hit</text><rect class="cell-fp" x="188" y="32" width="108" height="52" rx="2"/><text class="nt bad" x="242" y="56" text-anchor="middle">FP</text><text class="nm" x="242" y="70" text-anchor="middle">Type I error</text><rect class="cell-fn" x="76" y="94" width="108" height="52" rx="2"/><text class="nt bad" x="130" y="118" text-anchor="middle">FN</text><text class="nm" x="130" y="132" text-anchor="middle">Type II error</text><rect class="cell-tn" x="188" y="94" width="108" height="52" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="242" y="118" text-anchor="middle">TN</text><text class="nm" x="242" y="132" text-anchor="middle">correct pass</text><path class="edge" d="M296 58 H344" marker-end="url(#c)"/><text class="nt" x="352" y="46">Precision = TP / (TP + FP)</text><text class="lab" x="352" y="62">reads across the top row</text><text class="lab wn" x="352" y="78">use when a false alarm is costly</text><path class="edge" d="M130 146 V174 H344" marker-end="url(#c)"/><text class="nt" x="352" y="112">Recall = TP / (TP + FN)</text><text class="lab" x="352" y="128">reads down the left column</text><text class="lab wn" x="352" y="144">use when a miss is costly</text><text class="nt" x="352" y="176">F1 = 2PR / (P + R)</text><text class="lab" x="352" y="192">preferred when classes are unbalanced</text><text class="lab" x="76" y="200">Accuracy = (TP + TN) / all &#8212; misleading on unbalanced classes</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','Precision reads across the predicted-positive row; recall reads down the actual-positive column. Which one matters depends entirely on which error costs more. Accuracy looks appealing and is nearly useless when one class dominates &mdash; predicting the majority every time can score 95%.',ARRAY['QM.LM7.g']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM7.04','quant','ROC curve and AUC','Two ROC curves bowing toward the top left corner above a diagonal random-guessing line.','0 0 470 185','<line class="grid" x1="40" y1="26" x2="40" y2="138"/><line class="grid" x1="40" y1="138" x2="220" y2="138"/><text class="lab" x="130" y="153" text-anchor="middle">false positive rate</text><text class="lab" x="24" y="82" transform="rotate(-90 24 82)" text-anchor="middle">true positive rate</text><line x1="40" y1="138" x2="220" y2="26" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><path d="M40.0 138.0 L42.6 94.0 L45.1 86.8 L47.7 82.0 L50.3 78.3 L52.9 75.3 L55.4 72.8 L58.0 70.5 L60.6 68.5 L63.1 66.7 L65.7 65.0 L68.3 63.5 L70.9 62.0 L73.4 60.7 L76.0 59.4 L78.6 58.2 L81.1 57.1 L83.7 56.0 L86.3 54.9 L88.9 53.9 L91.4 53.0 L94.0 52.1 L96.6 51.2 L99.1 50.3 L101.7 49.5 L104.3 48.7 L106.9 47.9 L109.4 47.2 L112.0 46.4 L114.6 45.7 L117.1 45.0 L119.7 44.4 L122.3 43.7 L124.9 43.1 L127.4 42.5 L130.0 41.8 L132.6 41.2 L135.1 40.7 L137.7 40.1 L140.3 39.5 L142.9 39.0 L145.4 38.4 L148.0 37.9 L150.6 37.4 L153.1 36.9 L155.7 36.4 L158.3 35.9 L160.9 35.4 L163.4 34.9 L166.0 34.5 L168.6 34.0 L171.1 33.5 L173.7 33.1 L176.3 32.6 L178.9 32.2 L181.4 31.8 L184.0 31.4 L186.6 30.9 L189.1 30.5 L191.7 30.1 L194.3 29.7 L196.9 29.3 L199.4 29.0 L202.0 28.6 L204.6 28.2 L207.1 27.8 L209.7 27.4 L212.3 27.1 L214.9 26.7 L217.4 26.4 L220.0 26.0" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.8" fill="none"/><path d="M40.0 138.0 L42.6 124.6 L45.1 119.1 L47.7 114.8 L50.3 111.2 L52.9 108.1 L55.4 105.2 L58.0 102.6 L60.6 100.1 L63.1 97.8 L65.7 95.7 L68.3 93.6 L70.9 91.6 L73.4 89.7 L76.0 87.9 L78.6 86.2 L81.1 84.5 L83.7 82.8 L86.3 81.2 L88.9 79.6 L91.4 78.1 L94.0 76.7 L96.6 75.2 L99.1 73.8 L101.7 72.4 L104.3 71.1 L106.9 69.7 L109.4 68.4 L112.0 67.2 L114.6 65.9 L117.1 64.7 L119.7 63.5 L122.3 62.3 L124.9 61.1 L127.4 59.9 L130.0 58.8 L132.6 57.7 L135.1 56.6 L137.7 55.5 L140.3 54.4 L142.9 53.3 L145.4 52.3 L148.0 51.2 L150.6 50.2 L153.1 49.2 L155.7 48.2 L158.3 47.2 L160.9 46.2 L163.4 45.3 L166.0 44.3 L168.6 43.3 L171.1 42.4 L173.7 41.5 L176.3 40.5 L178.9 39.6 L181.4 38.7 L184.0 37.8 L186.6 36.9 L189.1 36.1 L191.7 35.2 L194.3 34.3 L196.9 33.4 L199.4 32.6 L202.0 31.7 L204.6 30.9 L207.1 30.1 L209.7 29.2 L212.3 28.4 L214.9 27.6 L217.4 26.8 L220.0 26.0" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/><text class="lab" x="236" y="44" fill="var(--cyan)">Model A &#8212; AUC 0.93</text><text class="lab" x="236" y="62" fill="var(--amber)">Model B &#8212; AUC 0.75</text><text class="lab" x="236" y="80" fill="var(--faint)">diagonal &#8212; AUC 0.50, random</text><text class="lab" x="236" y="106">the closer to the top-left corner,</text><text class="lab" x="236" y="120">the better the model</text><text class="lab wn" x="40" y="164">every point on a curve is one threshold choice &#8212; accepting more false positives buys more true positives</text>','Each point on a curve is a different classification threshold, so the curve traces the tradeoff rather than a single result. AUC summarises the whole curve: 0.5 is a coin flip, and area is what lets you compare two models without fixing a threshold first.',ARRAY['QM.LM7.g']::text[],'generated'),
('QM.LM7.05','quant','Model fit errors','Two diagnosis rows: high in-sample error meaning underfitting, and low in-sample with high out-of-sample error meaning overfitting, each paired with its remedy.','0 0 540 175','<text class="ttl" x="24" y="18">Which error do you have?</text><rect class="node-r" x="24" y="28" width="230" height="52" rx="2"/><text class="nt bad" x="38" y="48">High in-sample error</text><text class="lab" x="38" y="64">underfit &#8212; model too simple, bias error</text><rect class="node-r" x="24" y="88" width="230" height="52" rx="2"/><text class="nt wn" x="38" y="108">Low in-sample, high out-of-sample</text><text class="lab" x="38" y="124">overfit &#8212; model too complex, variance error</text><path class="edge" d="M254 54 H296" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M254 114 H296" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect class="node-ok" x="296" y="28" width="220" height="52" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="310" y="48">Add complexity or features</text><text class="lab" x="310" y="64">more IVs, non-linear model</text><rect class="node-ok" x="296" y="88" width="220" height="52" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="310" y="108">Regularise or add data</text><text class="lab" x="310" y="124">LASSO, prune, k-fold, bigger training set</text><text class="lab wn" x="24" y="164">RMSE compares models out of sample; the in/out gap tells you which direction to move</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','One comparison &mdash; in-sample against out-of-sample error &mdash; diagnoses the problem and points to the fix. The remedies are opposites, so getting the diagnosis backwards makes the model worse rather than merely failing to help.',ARRAY['QM.LM7.g']::text[],'generated')
